# Benchmark invocation

Full LFM pp512 (and similarly long multi-sample locked llama-bench) runs are expensive. Do **not**
start them by default, and never start one during ordinary coding, review, or handoff work.

## When a full locked pp512 is allowed

Only if one of these is true:

- Explicit user request for a full-model / pp512 / “full perf” run; or
- A PR/issue acceptance criterion that specifically requires locked pp512 evidence; or
- A change that cannot be validated cheaper **and** is expected to move end-to-end tok/s (after
  cheaper gates pass).

## Prefer this ladder

1. Unit/integration tests (`cargo test` / focused lib tests)
2. Criterion microbenches for host-side changes (quant, cache codec, hash, async reactor)
3. Short attribution/profile (tiny model, fewer repeats, or single warm iteration) for
   splits/unitaries/bindings/no-copy %
4. Single-sample or `r=1` pp512 only as a smoke — not the default “results” run
5. Full locked pp512 (`r≥3`, flock, full feature matrix) sparingly — typically once per milestone
   or when comparing to the published baseline (~160 tok/s develop / ~119 remediation / ~845 Vulkan)

## Also

- Never stack concurrent full benches; respect the global flock (`/tmp/ggml-npu-bench-global.lock`)
- Ensure shim / `compile_graph_v3` is current before any NPU full bench (avoid multi-hour invalid
  Vulkan-fallback runs)
- After a full run, update related GitHub issues with evidence; do not re-run to “confirm” without
  a code change
- PR #15 / handoff work: prefer unitary/split/program attribution counters and short profiles over
  another full pp512 unless the user asks
- Do not run locked fullperf inside a memory-stressed Cursor session; prefer a separate
  terminal/session. The LFM harness refuses high pre-existing swap and kills the bench session on
  low MemAvailable (`benchmarks/lib/lfm_process_guard.sh`)
